#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9564e999-9aa4-5f44-bc9b-d2aad049900a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "931ca260-f460-5126-a410-e7e929c16dd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13006" :question/problem "Given that $12 - 5m = 3m$, what is the value of $m?$" :question/worked-solution "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $-5m$ on the left-hand side and $3m$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $-5m$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{blue}5m}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-5m & =3m \\\\ 12-5m+5m & =3m+5m \\\\ 12 & =8m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =8m \\\\ \\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{8m}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}m}{\\cancel{8}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =m \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064345 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24a6a642-f1c3-5550-b40e-78fc6730bcf3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Three-Step Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "615f4a8c-a7d7-5533-badc-fc3d12678c95" :question/math-academy-id "e-13007" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3x-12 = 5x + 8.$" :question/worked-solution "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $3x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $3x$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}3x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-12 & =5x+8 \\\\ 3x-12-3x & =5x+8-3x \\\\ -12 & =2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $8$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{red}8}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =2x+8 \\\\ -12-8 & =2x+8-8 \\\\ -20 & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =2x \\\\ \\frac{-20}{2} & =\\frac{2x}{2} \\\\ -10 & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ -10 & =x \\\\ x & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063946 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75d27dfc-1297-5045-bedd-10e171209430" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Using the Distributive Law"}] :question/id #uuid "afb93968-87c1-5d28-8922-8143e6db77c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13008" :question/problem "Solve for $t$ where $2(t+3)= 5t-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses on the left-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t+3) & =5t-3 \\\\ 2\\cdot t+2\\cdot 3 & =5t-3 \\\\ 2t+6 & =5t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $2t$ on the left-hand side and $5t$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $2t$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}2t}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+6 & =5t-3 \\\\ 2t+6-2t & =5t-3-2t \\\\ 6 & =3t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}3}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =3t-3 \\\\ 6+3 & =3t-3+3 \\\\ 9 & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =3t \\\\ \\frac{9}{3} & =\\frac{3t}{3} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}t}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 3 & =t \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75d27dfc-1297-5045-bedd-10e171209430" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Using the Distributive Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97df5479-d2b0-51aa-9041-5aedd72c0572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486937} {:answer/id #uuid "bda8435a-7a84-5fe5-a5b2-1c1d85d9ffb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487009} {:answer/id #uuid "beee8b78-e76d-56ea-9b45-1f2c2a477fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487016} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ad57e0-f1ac-5782-a28e-0a1fd9ded8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487035} {:answer/id #uuid "e218fb36-792f-5896-b552-14a611cc36ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97df5479-d2b0-51aa-9041-5aedd72c0572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486937} :answer-field/id #uuid "99db8cd8-808a-5259-ace5-b8b11ffde566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3b531c0-4761-5340-bef2-0f6d109a9d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-128811" :question/problem "If $-3(1-p)=4(p+1),$ then $p=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(1-p) & =4(p+1) \\\\ -3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot p & =4\\cdot p+4\\cdot 1 \\\\ -3+3p & =4p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $3p$ on the left-hand side and $4p$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $3p$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $3p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+3p & =4p+4 \\\\ -3+3p-3p & =4p+4-3p \\\\ -3 & =p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we remove the $4$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p+4 \\\\ -3-4 & =p+4-4 \\\\ -7 & =p \\\\ p & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9564e999-9aa4-5f44-bc9b-d2aad049900a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211c0227-f141-5547-8df5-6f000d316d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486660} {:answer/id #uuid "aaabe82f-22e2-55ae-b96c-536137e02bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486980} {:answer/id #uuid "bf52af84-16cf-5a2b-8555-d2d86b48cbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487017} {:answer/id #uuid "cea7669f-2849-5f84-80a5-204847dbd3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487049} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d471ad-7459-5b1e-8190-51d9b384fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d471ad-7459-5b1e-8190-51d9b384fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487090} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d273506-c893-5e80-8659-3acbac14152b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96ac96b0-7a48-5300-b28c-ed2048f4b199" :question/math-academy-id "q-130790" :question/problem "Given that $2x+6=5x$, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $2x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $2x$ from the left-hand side. We do this by subtracting $2x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & =5x \\\\ 2x+6-2x & =5x-2x \\\\ 6 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064346 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75d27dfc-1297-5045-bedd-10e171209430" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Using the Distributive Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c871f6cd-7abf-5a9a-b2ad-5ea06b43b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c871f6cd-7abf-5a9a-b2ad-5ea06b43b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487040} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1c4692-78ee-52dd-9ff4-165a30f837b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f616f4fb-ccb3-5ccf-8dc2-aabb1a45bb95" :question/math-academy-id "q-171587" :question/problem "If $7(x-2)=3(x+2)+2x,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x-2) & =3(x+2)+2x \\\\ 7\\cdot x-7\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x+3\\cdot 2+2x \\\\ 7x-14 & =3x+6+2x \\\\ 7x-14 & =6+3x+2x \\\\ 7x-14 & =6+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $7x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $5x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $5x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-14 & =6+5x \\\\ 7x-14-5x & =6+5x-5x \\\\ 2x-14 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-14$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding $14$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-14 & =6 \\\\ 2x-14+14 & =6+14 \\\\ 2x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =20 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{20}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9564e999-9aa4-5f44-bc9b-d2aad049900a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d37966e-130a-5268-9c32-6546a6d15788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d37966e-130a-5268-9c32-6546a6d15788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486900} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ca13b4-3c1e-5cd7-bfa9-e8ddd9f051e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee568a57-5aea-5924-963c-e0b1b71db69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181250" :question/problem "If $17y=9y-16$, then $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $17y$ on the left-hand side and $9y$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $9y$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting $9y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17y & =9y-16 \\\\ 17y-9y & =9y-16-9y \\\\ 8y & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y & =-16 \\\\ \\frac{8y}{8} & =\\frac{(-16)}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}y}{\\cancel{8}} & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064345 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24a6a642-f1c3-5550-b40e-78fc6730bcf3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Three-Step Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40237f1b-8849-5877-8807-fb4c39559aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40237f1b-8849-5877-8807-fb4c39559aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486733} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e87f2e3-5590-504a-8ee6-06b140ad42d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b03a4399-665a-560d-811a-3bccaf2d3f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-181274" :question/problem "If $10x-3=5+6x,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $10x$ on the left-hand side and $6x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $6x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $6x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-3 & =5+6x \\\\ 10x-3-6x & =5+6x-6x \\\\ 4x-3 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3 & =5 \\\\ 4x-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ 4x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =8 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064345 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24a6a642-f1c3-5550-b40e-78fc6730bcf3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Three-Step Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989a15e6-9b7d-5dca-a7cb-1f8d1488a7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "989a15e6-9b7d-5dca-a7cb-1f8d1488a7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486939} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec197e84-c7a7-5544-ba1a-18d3c2d6e2d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a36c0516-4a73-5ecf-b71f-434626a39f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181286" :question/problem "If $-4-3t=3t-2,$ then $t=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $-3t$ on the left-hand side and $3t$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $-3t$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by adding $3t$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-3t & =3t-2 \\\\ -4-3t+3t & =3t-2+3t \\\\ -4 & =6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-2$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =6t-2 \\\\ -4+2 & =6t-2+2 \\\\ -2 & =6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =6t \\\\ \\frac{(-2)}{6} & =\\frac{6t}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\cancel{6}t}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =t \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
